/T (Course details: Related themes in the course treat conflict and stress as normal parts of life that are best handled head-on. A conflict is simply a disagreement; it turns destructive when communication fails and constructive when it is managed well. The lectures move from negotiation to mediation and arbitration, always aiming for a win-win outcome, and ask learners to stand firm on their needs rather than their fixed demands. Conflict is grouped into intrapersonal, interpersonal and intergroup types. Stress is defined as a gap between what a situation demands and what a person can supply, separating helpful stress \(eustress\) from harmful distress.)
/E (Course details: Related themes in the course treat conflict and stress as normal parts of life that are best handled head-on. A conflict is simply a disagreement; it turns destructive when communication fails and constructive when it is managed well. The lectures move from negotiation to mediation and arbitration, always aiming for a win-win outcome, and ask learners to stand firm on their needs rather than their fixed demands. Conflict is grouped into intrapersonal, interpersonal and intergroup types. Stress is defined as a gap between what a situation demands and what a person can supply, separating helpful stress \(eustress\) from harmful distress.)
